      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fair Play got its name from a nineteenth-century tradition where a bystander urged two brawling settlers to play fair.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fair_play_south_carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The community of Fair Play is home to the Upstate Heritage Quilt Trail, which emphasizes the area's agricultural heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fair_play_south_carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Fair Play features preserved landmarks including the Fair Play Presbyterian Church, which was established in 1903.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fair_play_south_carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lake Hartwell borders Fair Play to the west and south, providing extensive recreational access for boating and fishing.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fair_play_south_carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Early white settlement in Fair Play began in the late eighteenth century following the American Revolutionary War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
